<p>A primitive data type is one built directly into the C compiler. The four basic primitive types are:</p><ul><li><strong>int</strong>: Used to store integers. Typically occupies 4 bytes of memory (on modern architectures), spanning values from -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647.</li><li><strong>char</strong>: Used to store single characters. Occupies exactly 1 byte of memory and maps characters to integer values using the ASCII table (e.g. ''A'' represents value 65).</li><li><strong>float</strong>: Used to store single-precision fractional/floating-point values. Occupies 4 bytes.</li><li><strong>double</strong>: Used to store double-precision floating-point values. Occupies 8 bytes and offers greater precision.</li></ul>');

